            <ArticleTitle>Local anesthetic lidocaine delivery system: chitosan and hyaluronic acid-modified layer-by-layer lipid nanoparticles.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>3529-3537</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Transdermal local anesthesia is one of the most applied strategies to avoid systemic adverse effects; there is an appealing need for a prolonged local anesthetic that would provide better bioavailability and longer pain relief with a single administration.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Layer-by-layer (LBL) technique was used in this study to explore a nanosized drug delivery system for local anesthetic therapy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">LBL-coated lidocaine-loaded nanostructured lipid nanoparticles (LBL-LA/NLCs) were prepared and characterized in terms of particle size (PS), zeta potential, drug encapsulation efficiency (EE), in vitro skin permeation and in vivo local anesthetic studies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Evaluation of the in vitro skin permeation and in vivo anesthesia effect illustrated that LBL-LA/NLCs can enhance and prolong the anesthetic effect of LA.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">LBL-LA/NLCs could function as a promising drug delivery strategy for overcoming the barrier function of the skin and could deliver anesthetic through the skin with sustained release behavior for local anesthetic therapy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
